#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19d1448c-d5fd-5615-b657-ff4d66867834" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "67da2ffa-b472-5084-985c-dde4cd2df661" :question/math-academy-id "e-5219" :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](images/57/57ec3c089f80e2d35d9b83f6791eefca9b452d933f82209ed434d35d77b78788.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(1,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=5,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087532 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087833 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e19ee3-db9a-58d7-8ca3-4e8801aee656" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Tall Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "e01914e2-b4f0-52b5-baf1-8b7fa5da3f18" :question/math-academy-id "e-5222" :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](images/b6/b6eb2c0222181eaa4eb2a1c232fdf2eee905ab0e0a8a9c2284911ab8dd26cade.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087834 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5028940-be34-56b7-8947-6129252f06a7" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Center and Lengths of the Major and Minor Axes Given the Equation of an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "10dda4fa-e047-53f4-a20a-b881e14f3e2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5223" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{25} + \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1,$ find its center, and the lengths of its major and minor axes." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=-2,$ so the center is located at $(2,-2).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have $a^2=25$ and $b^2=16.$\n\n- Since $a^2=25,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25} = 5.$\n\n- Since $b^2=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16} = 4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a = 2\\cdot5 = 10,$ and the minor axis has length $2b = 2\\cdot4 = 8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087832 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "19d1448c-d5fd-5615-b657-ff4d66867834" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Wide Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7bb57990-39f1-562f-bbb9-67d776c7f19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bb57990-39f1-562f-bbb9-67d776c7f19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293429} :answer-field/id #uuid "03acd711-49d1-5efa-b980-82c65670af6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9f27b41-29b4-5ec4-8310-cd993b30c28c" :question/math-academy-id "q-278255" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c95243ea94951012ec14c6f16b4276a591c10c22798efc82f73bcd9d45e65ae.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-4,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=5,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-4))^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+4)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
